WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:07.940
The United States Postal Service is now thinking
about closing post offices around the country

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:13.829
ahead of a presidential election in which
we are anticipating more people than ever

00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:19.030
will vote by mail because of this thing called
the global corona virus pandemic, which has

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.340
been acutely mishandled by Donald Trump.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.150
Now, we're going to be talking today about
a lot of angles on vote by mail, including

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:27.070
how to vote by mail.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.840
But this is, I think, where it makes sense
to start.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.449
We knew when it looked like vote by mail was
going to be a big deal this November that

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:36.379
the post office was going to be a target.

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:39.789
And part of the reason why is Republicans
love going after the post office even when

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.199
there's not a pandemic.

00:00:41.199 --> 00:00:44.039
And when people are not particularly planning
to vote by mail.

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:47.910
Trump loves talking about how the post office
is inefficient.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.600
Republicans love talking about privatization.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.019
U.P.S., FedEx, they're better than the post
office.

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:57.739
The post office is kind of a perfect target
for people like Donald Trump and many Republicans

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:02.710
because you can talk about privatizing while
shrinking government while attacking government

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:07.030
inefficiency and saying stuff like if the
post office can't even get it right.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.820
How could we possibly have the government
handle health care?

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.510
That's often the way they like to talk about
it.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.921
And then while we were already suspicious
about Trump's plans for the post office, Trump

00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:22.460
goes and appoints a huge donor from his campaign
donor to his campaign, rather, Lewis Dejoy

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:28.300
as postmaster general, who immediately starts
talking about reevaluating the post office.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.710
Talking about a fresh look at the post office,
limiting overtime hours for postal workers.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.020
And then we get a clearer picture of what
the plan is.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.600
We've now heard from Democratic Senator Joe
Manchin of West Virginia that he has been

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:48.390
receiving letters that there are plans for
closures of post offices in the state of West

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.830
Virginia, which may just be the very beginning
of this strategy.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:57.680
When Manchin sent a letter to Trump's new
postmaster general to essentially say, what

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.130
on earth is going on here, he got some vague
response similar to what we already talked

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:04.130
about.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.159
You know, we need to take a fresh look at
how the post office should be run.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:11.890
Now, I actually found a document from the
Congressional Research Service called Common

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:15.670
Questions about post office closures.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.840
And it explains how and under what circumstances
post office branches can be closed.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.290
And when you look at this document on page
nine, it says, what is that process?

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:33.510
And the the federal law around closing post
offices includes that there must be adequate

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:40.209
notice of the intention to close or consolidate
branches at least 60 days prior to the proposed

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.640
date of the closure.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.360
And if a decision is made to close a post
office, everybody served by that post office

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.380
must be notified.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.190
And the post office has to wait at least 60
more days before proceeding with the closure.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:59.610
Anybody who is served by a post office that
may be closing has the right to appeal the

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:04.020
closure, and there will be up to 120 days
to consider the appeal.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:09.290
So if you just look at the calendar, it's
already too late to close post offices before

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.340
the election.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.730
By the normal process, how can you get around
this?

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:17.990
I'm guessing you understand the way this is
going to go.

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:18.990
Emergency's.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:24.790
There is something called an emergency suspension
process, but the situations where you can

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:29.780
use such a process to close a post office
branch is very, very limited.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.070
These are limited situations.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:36.311
For example, if the post office is in a building
that you rent from, you know, a landlord,

00:03:36.311 --> 00:03:42.050
a private landlord and the landlord closes
the building as an example, you would be able

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.300
to close the post office.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.080
The post office would have no choice in that
case.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.020
But the close sooner than the normal timeline.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.730
But right now, we certainly don't meet that
standard.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.209
And if kids are going back to school, you
can't really argue that it's safe for kids

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:56.690
to be in school, but post offices have to
close.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.570
So here's the plan that they're cooking up.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:05.250
Cut overtime if carriers can't finish their
daily deliveries without taking overtime.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.849
They have to bring the undelivered mail back
to the post office the next day.

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:13.050
They've got to take out the new mail plus
the leftover stuff from day one.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.660
Can't finish it by 5:00 p.m. Bring it all
back to the post office.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:21.600
Day three, you now have to deliver that day's
mail, plus the leftovers from day one and

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.050
the leftovers from day two.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:24.410
I think you understand.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:25.630
That's part of the plan.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.630
And meanwhile, look at whether.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:34.389
They might be able to shut down some branches
as Donald Trump continues to publicly assail

00:04:34.389 --> 00:04:38.710
the ability of the post office to deliver
absentee ballots.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.230
Now, that's the plan.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.460
It doesn't match up with reality.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.340
And the Postal Service has actually said not
Trump's postmaster, but others in the Postal

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.840
Service have said, we have that, we've got
this.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.940
We can handle the the ballots.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.750
That's really not a big deal for us.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.880
Donald Trump, however, is going to continue
casting doubt about that.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:06:22.169
And that's what we're going to talk about
next.

